{
  "id": "devops-184",
  "title": "AWS DynamoDB - Insert Data Using AWS Lambda - GeeksforGeeks",
  "content": "<div class=\"html-chunk\"><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>AWS Lambda can be integrated with DynamoDB to build scalable serverless applications. In this setup, Lambda executes Python code, IAM manages permissions, and DynamoDB stores the application data. Its architecture components are:</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><b><strong>Lambda:</strong></b><span> Executes the Python code</span></li><li value=\"2\"><b><strong>IAM Role:</strong></b><span> Grants permissions to access DynamoDB</span></li><li value=\"3\"><b><strong>DynamoDB:</strong></b><span> Stores the data items</span></li></ul><h2 id=\"implementation\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Implementation</span></h2><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 1: Sign in to your AWS account at the AWS Management Console.</strong></b></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 2: Create a DynamoDB Table</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Search for DynamoDB in the AWS services search bar and open it.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Click Create table</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Enter Table name and Partition key</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>Optionally add a sort key if required.</span></li><li value=\"5\"><span>Click Create table</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936\" height=\"1413\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-1.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936.png 1661w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604095948029902/Screenshot-2026-06-04-095936-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"1661\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><blockquote><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Note:</strong></b><span> The partition key you define here must be included in every </span><code><span>put_item</span></code><span> call from your Lambda function.</span></p></blockquote><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 3: Create an IAM Role for Lambda</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Search for IAM in the AWS console</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Navigate to Access Management → Roles</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Click Create role</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232\" height=\"1399\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-2.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232.png 2875w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100427637237/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100232-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2875\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 4: Configure Trusted Entity</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Select AWS service as the trusted entity type.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Select Lambda as the use case.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Click Next.</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723\" height=\"1540\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-3.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723.png 2878w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100739454760/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100723-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2878\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Attach Permissions</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Search for and attach \"AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess\"</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Click Next</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858\" height=\"1050\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-4.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858.png 2838w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604100929917569/Screenshot-2026-06-04-100858-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2838\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Name the Role</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Enter a role name</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Click Create role</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053\" height=\"1368\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-5.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053.png 2874w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101109344429/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101053-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2874\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><blockquote><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Note:</strong></b><span> For production systems, follow the Principle of Least Privilege and grant only the specific DynamoDB actions your function requires (e.g., </span><code><span>dynamodb:PutItem</span></code><span>) instead of full access.</span></p></blockquote><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 5: Create the Lambda Function</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Navigate to AWS Lambda.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Click Create function.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Select Author from scratch.</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>Enter Function name</span></li><li value=\"5\"><span>Choose Runtime Python 3.x</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623\" height=\"1480\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-6.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623.png 2873w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604101638424022/Screenshot-2026-06-04-101623-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2873\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 6: Configure Execution Role</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Expand Additional settings while creating the Lambda function.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Open Custom execution role.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Select Use an existing role.</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>Choose the IAM role created earlier.</span></li><li value=\"5\"><span>Click on Save</span></li><li value=\"6\"><span>Click Create function.</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516\" height=\"1595\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-7.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516.png 2819w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604102527579616/Screenshot-2026-06-04-102516-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2819\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 7: Add Python Code</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Open the Code tab of the Lambda function.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Replace the default Lambda handler code with the following:</span></li></ul><gfg-tabs data-mode=\"light\" data-run-ide=\"false\">\n            <gfg-tab slot=\"tab\">Python</gfg-tab>\n<gfg-panel slot=\"panel\" data-code-lang=\"python3\" data-main-code-start=\"None\" data-main-code-end=\"None\">\n    <code class=\"language-python3\"><div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">json</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"nn\">boto3</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span> <span class=\"nf\">lambda_handler</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">event</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">context</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n\n    <span class=\"n\">dynamodb</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">boto3</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">resource</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s1\">'dynamodb'</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"c1\"># Replace 'sample' with your DynamoDB table name</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">table</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">dynamodb</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Table</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s1\">'sample'</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"n\">response</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">table</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">put_item</span><span class=\"p\">(</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">Item</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n            <span class=\"s1\">'empid'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">101</span><span class=\"p\">,</span>\n            <span class=\"s1\">'name'</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"s1\">'Samar'</span>\n        <span class=\"p\">}</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">response</span>\n</pre></div></code>\n</gfg-panel></gfg-tabs><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057\" height=\"1502\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-8.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057.png 2875w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104112433322/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104057-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2875\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 8: Deploy and Test the Function</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Click Deploy to save the code.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Open the Test tab.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Create a new test event.</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>Click Test to invoke the Lambda function.</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333\" height=\"1146\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-9.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333.png 2255w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104420413398/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104333-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2255\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: left;\"><b><strong>Step 9: Verify Data in DynamoDB Table</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Open DynamoDB from the AWS Console. </span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Select your table from the list. </span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Navigate to the Explore table items tab. </span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606\" height=\"860\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-10.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606.png 2877w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104853280473/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104606-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2877\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Click Scan or Run to view the table data.</span></li></ul><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930\" height=\"1181\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"./images/devops-184-img-11.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930.png 2874w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-300.png 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-660.png 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-768.png 768w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20260604104955591583/Screenshot-2026-06-04-104930-1024.png 1024w\" width=\"2874\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><h3 id=\"troubleshooting-common-errors\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Troubleshooting Common Errors</span></h3><table><thead><tr><th><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Error</span></p></th><th><span>Cause</span></th><th><span>Solution</span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>AccessDeniedException</span></p></th><td><span>Missing IAM permissions.</span></td><td><span>Add \"AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess\" (or \"dynamodb:PutItem\") to Lambda's execution role under Configuration &gt; Permissions.</span></td></tr><tr><th><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>ResourceNotFoundException</span></p></th><td><span>Wrong table name.</span></td><td><span>Check \"dynamodb.Table('name')\". Match the DynamoDB table name exactly (case-sensitive).</span></td></tr><tr><th><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>ValidationException</span></p></th><td><span>Missing partition key.</span></td><td><span>Ensure your \"</span><code><span>put_item\"</span></code><span> call includes the exact partition key defined during table creation.</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
  "textContent": "AWS Lambda can be integrated with DynamoDB to build scalable serverless applications. In this setup, Lambda executes Python code, IAM manages permissions, and DynamoDB stores the application data. Its architecture components are:Lambda: Executes the Python codeIAM Role: Grants permissions to access DynamoDBDynamoDB: Stores the data itemsImplementationStep 1: Sign in to your AWS account at the AWS Management Console.Step 2: Create a DynamoDB TableSearch for DynamoDB in the AWS services search bar and open it.Click Create tableEnter Table name and Partition keyOptionally add a sort key if required.Click Create table Note: The partition key you define here must be included in every put_item call from your Lambda function.Step 3: Create an IAM Role for LambdaSearch for IAM in the AWS consoleNavigate to Access Management → RolesClick Create role Step 4: Configure Trusted EntitySelect AWS service as the trusted entity type.Select Lambda as the use case.Click Next. Attach PermissionsSearch for and attach \"AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess\"Click Next Name the RoleEnter a role nameClick Create role Note: For production systems, follow the Principle of Least Privilege and grant only the specific DynamoDB actions your function requires (e.g., dynamodb:PutItem) instead of full access.Step 5: Create the Lambda FunctionNavigate to AWS Lambda.Click Create function.Select Author from scratch.Enter Function nameChoose Runtime Python 3.x Step 6: Configure Execution RoleExpand Additional settings while creating the Lambda function.Open Custom execution role.Select Use an existing role.Choose the IAM role created earlier.Click on SaveClick Create function. Step 7: Add Python CodeOpen the Code tab of the Lambda function.Replace the default Lambda handler code with the following:\n            Python\n\n    import json\nimport boto3\n\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n\n    dynamodb = boto3.resource('dynamodb')\n\n    # Replace 'sample' with your DynamoDB table name\n    table = dynamodb.Table('sample')\n\n    response = table.put_item(\n        Item={\n            'empid': 101,\n            'name': 'Samar'\n        }\n    )\n\n    return response\n\n Step 8: Deploy and Test the FunctionClick Deploy to save the code.Open the Test tab.Create a new test event.Click Test to invoke the Lambda function. Step 9: Verify Data in DynamoDB TableOpen DynamoDB from the AWS Console. Select your table from the list. Navigate to the Explore table items tab.  Click Scan or Run to view the table data. Troubleshooting Common ErrorsErrorCauseSolutionAccessDeniedExceptionMissing IAM permissions.Add \"AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess\" (or \"dynamodb:PutItem\") to Lambda's execution role under Configuration > Permissions.ResourceNotFoundExceptionWrong table name.Check \"dynamodb.Table('name')\". Match the DynamoDB table name exactly (case-sensitive).ValidationExceptionMissing partition key.Ensure your \"put_item\" call includes the exact partition key defined during table creation.",
  "url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/aws-dynamodb-insert-data-using-aws-lambda"
}
